After /cd, the file tools (yours and sub-agents') now refuse paths under the new directory's [directories] deny entries for the rest of the session. Before this, only its [permissions] deny applied (to bash, since Day 218). It's committed as 863e2ae5 ("Part of #869"), not pushed yet. Build, all 6456 tests, clippy with -D warnings, fmt check and the module-size gate all pass.

How it works
• **Relative entries:** `deny = ["secrets"]` is turned into `<that dir>/secrets` at the moment of the `/cd`, so it keeps pointing there after a later `/cd`. A test covers this, including that secrets/key.txt relative to the current working directory is not refused.
• **Matching:** the check reuses the existing startup matcher (`DirectoryRestrictions::check_path`), so symlinks, wildcards and paths that can't be resolved behave exactly as they do at startup. There's no second matcher.
• **Guards:** `tool_wrappers.rs` has exactly two places that check the `path` argument, and both now check the startup fence first, then the `/cd` list. A deliberately weak source test pins that count at 2.
• **The list:** it only grows and is never cleared, and a new `/cd` appends to it. With an empty list the guard gives exactly the same results as before (full-equality test). The list, the refusal function and all tests live in cd_config_note.rs. tool_wrappers.rs grew 6 lines and dispatch.rs grew 2.
• **The `/cd warning** now says [directories] deny is applied to the file tools and that bash never reads it. It also names [directories] allow as still not applied. I updated the four affected existing tests in place without weakening any, and a [directories]`-deny-only config still prints the yellow warning.
• **Positive control:** I made the refusal always return `None`, and exactly the two refusal tests failed by name. I restored it in the same command, and no sabotage marker remains.
• Docs: the "After /cd" section of docs/src/configuration/permissions.md already described this behaviour, so I rewrote it.

Still not done (posted on #869):
• **Not reloaded on /cd:** `[directories] allow` (it would have to be combined with the startup allow list, which is a design decision), `[permissions] allow`, hooks, MCP servers and skills.
• **Not checked against the /cd list:** 6 places outside the file tools still check paths against the startup fence only: `@path` mentions and `/add`, the project-context loaders, and code in commands_project.rs, commands_rename.rs, commands_map.rs and commands_goal.rs. So a file in a /cd-denied directory is refused by read_file but can still be pulled in with an @-mention. That's the obvious next slice.

Two corrections I made to my own write-up:
• The first commit message said 7 places still use only the startup fence. One of those was a test helper, so I amended the commit to say 6.
• The issue comment first cited the commit hash from before the amend. I edited it to 863e2ae5.

